#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186293760 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0236acfd-59d1-5bc8-9e60-1c76ddc536f5" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Quadratic Equation Given a Complex Root and Its Leading Coefficient"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ba1033dd-87e2-53bc-ac90-06f691500078" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x^2-2\\sqrt{2}x+11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293762}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ba1033dd-87e2-53bc-ac90-06f691500078" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x^2-2\\sqrt{2}x+11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293762} :answer-field/id #uuid "0d243446-722f-54af-b066-db2a2530ec83" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293761}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4773acbb-4d68-5273-91de-779cf2e7db02" :question/math-academy-id "q-281349" :question/problem "A quadratic equation with real coefficients has a complex root $x=\\sqrt{2}+3\\text{i}.$ Given that the coefficient of ${x}^{2}$ in the equation is $1,$ find the equation. Write your answer in the form $a{x}^{2}+bx+c=0,$ where $a,$ $b,$ and $c$ are real constants.\n\n{{field-1}}$=0$" :question/worked-solution "Since the quadratic equation has real coefficients and $x=\\sqrt{2}+3\\text{i}$ is a root, we know that its complex conjugate $x=\\sqrt{2}-3\\text{i}$ is also a root. Therefore, using the zero product property, we can write a quadratic equation with the required roots as follows:\n\n$$\n(x-(\\sqrt{2}+3\\text{i}))(x-(\\sqrt{2}-3\\text{i}))=0\n$$\n\nBy expanding the parentheses, we can write the equation in standard form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-x(\\sqrt{2}+3\\text{i})-x(\\sqrt{2}-3\\text{i})+(\\sqrt{2}+3\\text{i})(\\sqrt{2}-3\\text{i}) & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-\\sqrt{2}x-\\cancel{3x\\text{i}}-\\sqrt{2}x+\\cancel{3x\\text{i}}+2-9{\\text{i}}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-2\\sqrt{2}\\,x+2+9 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-2\\sqrt{2}\\,x+11 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186293763 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0236acfd-59d1-5bc8-9e60-1c76ddc536f5" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Quadratic Equation Given a Complex Root and Its Leading Coefficient"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "af9a87dc-d1fb-5866-96a8-3e45c6e4e2a4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5x^2-20x+35" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293765}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "af9a87dc-d1fb-5866-96a8-3e45c6e4e2a4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5x^2-20x+35" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293765} :answer-field/id #uuid "ce3ef4f6-1742-5d49-8b20-c2b99adb9979" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293764}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "42d734ce-bfbe-599a-be22-d761336041fc" :question/math-academy-id "q-281362" :question/problem "A quadratic equation in the variable $x$ with real coefficients has a root $x=2+\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{i}.$ Given that the coefficient of ${x}^{2}$ in the equation is $5,$ find the equation. Write your answer in the form $a{x}^{2}+bx+c=0,$ where $a,$ $b,$ and $c$ are real constants.\n\n{{field-1}}$=0$" :question/worked-solution "Since the quadratic equation has real coefficients and $x=2+\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{i}$ is a root, its conjugate $x=2-\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{i}$ must also be a root. Therefore, using the zero product property, we can write a quadratic equation with the required roots as follows:\n\n$$\n(x-(2+\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{i}))(x-(2-\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{i}))=0\n$$\n\nBy expanding the parentheses, we can write the equation in standard form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-x(2+\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{i})-x(2-\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{i})+(2+\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{i})(2-\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{i}) & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-2x-\\cancel{x\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{i}}-2x+\\cancel{x\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{i}}+4-3{\\text{i}}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-4x+4+3 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-4x+7 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation has roots $x=2\\pm \\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{i},$ but the coefficient of ${x}^{2}$ is not $5.$ Therefore, to find the desired equation, we multiply the equation above by $5.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5({x}^{2}-4x+7) & =0 \\\\ 5{x}^{2}-20x+35 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186293766 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0db14c2a-b6c3-5550-8ff6-c1a9e2b4ea5d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Quadratic Equation Given an Imaginary Root and Its Leading Coefficient"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fd5a95af-d156-5c01-9ba0-71687eee251d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5{x}^{2}-9=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293768} {:answer/id #uuid "9f692cb6-856d-5e41-a94c-cbdac9cbf53c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5{x}^{2}+9=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293769} {:answer/id #uuid "06033648-2923-53e5-98a7-5d2d2b513459" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5{x}^{2}+5=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293770} {:answer/id #uuid "f9d82ba7-7e37-53ce-a6c6-3de2e8424366" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5{x}^{2}-45=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293771} {:answer/id #uuid "28fe5973-cb88-55f0-9c01-504131fb6482" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5{x}^{2}+45=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293772}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f9d82ba7-7e37-53ce-a6c6-3de2e8424366" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5{x}^{2}-45=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293771} :answer-field/id #uuid "04e8d433-e940-54f7-bfae-76a9b882a830" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293767}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "86f6b095-6a7b-53c1-a17a-97b2e5ee73eb" :question/math-academy-id "q-28520" :question/problem "A quadratic equation in the variable $x$ with real coefficients has a root $x=-3\\text{i}.$ Given that the coefficient of ${x}^{2}$ in the equation is $-5$, find the equation." :question/worked-solution "Since the quadratic equation has real coefficients and  $x=-3\\text{i}$ is a root, its conjugate  $x=3\\text{i}$ must also be a root. Therefore, using the zero product property, we can write a quadratic equation with the required roots as follows:\n\n$$\n(x-3\\text{i})(x+3\\text{i})=0\n$$\n\nMultiplying out the parentheses and simplifying gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-(3\\text{i})^{2} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-9(-1) & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+9 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation has roots $x=\\pm 3\\text{i},$ but the coefficient of ${x}^{2}$ is not $-5.$ Therefore, to find the desired equation, we multiply the equation above by $-5.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-5({x}^{2}+9) & =0 \\\\ -5{x}^{2}-45 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115613 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0db14c2a-b6c3-5550-8ff6-c1a9e2b4ea5d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Quadratic Equation Given an Imaginary Root and Its Leading Coefficient"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8564bc3a-5796-58f4-8c03-ef3893f0a46c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2x^{2} + x - 2 = 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113079} {:answer/id #uuid "a7de3a7b-8522-5500-abe9-78b767c73afa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2x^{2} - 2 = 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113080} {:answer/id #uuid "aa189dba-e2e7-5e42-a9b4-a36ad1ac7050" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2x^{2} + 1 = 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113081} {:answer/id #uuid "d4a8f90f-608a-561c-9c38-d4d5df7149d7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2x^{2} + x - 4 = 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113082} {:answer/id #uuid "1a75ff1f-0fa3-55be-ac7b-5819e16c580c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2x^{2} - 1 = 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113083}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a7de3a7b-8522-5500-abe9-78b767c73afa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2x^{2} - 2 = 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113080} :answer-field/id #uuid "7b9b2732-b5ad-5bb8-9f15-f028b82541ec" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113078}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ca36dd1b-a9c8-5277-83cd-01acbe618070" :question/math-academy-id "q-28524" :question/problem "A quadratic equation in the variable $x$ with real coefficients has a root $x=-\\text{i}.$ Given that the coefficient of ${x}^{2}$ in the equation is $-2$, find the equation." :question/worked-solution "Since the quadratic equation has real coefficients and  $x=-\\text{i}$ is a root, its conjugate  $x=\\text{i}$ must also be a root. Therefore, using the zero product property, we can write a quadratic equation with the required roots as follows:\n\n$$\n(x-\\text{i})(x+\\text{i})=0\n$$\n\nMultiplying out the parentheses and simplifying gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-(\\text{i})^{2} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-(-1) & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+1 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation has roots $x=\\pm \\text{i},$ but the coefficient of ${x}^{2}$ is not $-2.$ Therefore, to find the desired equation, we multiply the equation above by $-2.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2({x}^{2}+1) & =0 \\\\ -2{x}^{2}-2 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186293773 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0db14c2a-b6c3-5550-8ff6-c1a9e2b4ea5d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Quadratic Equation Given an Imaginary Root and Its Leading Coefficient"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "75ec5e80-fa08-513c-9949-e9f4db637821" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5{x}^{2}+36=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293775} {:answer/id #uuid "5b0316d5-e883-5c31-95ca-96805a19136d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5{x}^{2}-36=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293776} {:answer/id #uuid "ccd1a6fa-ece0-538a-bf74-66a6f8e7380c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5{x}^{2}+6=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293777} {:answer/id #uuid "e5a41398-f1a7-5432-9465-da17943fa0b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5{x}^{2}+x-6=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293778} {:answer/id #uuid "47db38ef-94d6-524d-b228-ca247ffdcee7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5{x}^{2}+2x+36=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293779}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "75ec5e80-fa08-513c-9949-e9f4db637821" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5{x}^{2}+36=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293775} :answer-field/id #uuid "83039dd7-8a14-516b-9035-ef61fada170d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293774}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "1dbe97a1-f845-5777-85dd-485d7d65218d" :question/math-academy-id "q-8186" :question/problem "A quadratic equation in the variable $x$ with real coefficients has a root $x=-\\,\\frac{6\\text{i}}{\\sqrt{5}}.$ Given that the coefficient of ${x}^{2}$ in the equation is $5,$ find the equation." :question/worked-solution "Since the quadratic equation has real coefficients and  $x=-\\,\\frac{6\\text{i}}{\\sqrt{5}}$ is a root, its conjugate  $x=\\frac{6\\text{i}}{\\sqrt{5}}$ must also be a root. Therefore, using the zero product property, we can write a quadratic equation with the required roots as follows:\n\n$$\n(x-\\frac{6\\text{i}}{\\sqrt{5}})(x+\\frac{6\\text{i}}{\\sqrt{5}})=0\n$$\n\nMultiplying out the parentheses and simplifying gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-{(\\frac{6\\text{i}}{\\sqrt{5}})}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-\\frac{36}{5}(-1) & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+\\frac{36}{5} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation has roots $x=\\pm \\frac{6\\text{i}}{\\sqrt{5}},$ but the coefficient of ${x}^{2}$ is not $5.$ Therefore, to find the desired equation, we multiply the equation above by $5.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5({x}^{2}+\\frac{36}{5}) & =0 \\\\ 5{x}^{2}+36 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
